===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pent-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|path}}, {{m|enm|peth}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pæþ||path, track}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*paþ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*paþaz||path}}. The Proto-Germanic term is possibly borrowed from {{der|en|ira}}, from {{der|en|ira-pro|*pántaHh}}, from {{der|en|iir-pro|*pántaHs}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*póntoh₁s}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*pent-|t=to pass}}, however this is disputed. Cognate with {{cog|fy|paad}}, {{cog|nl|pad}}, {{cog|de|Pfad}}. Indo-Iranian cognates could be {{cog|ae|𐬞𐬀𐬧‎𐬙𐬃|t=way}}, {{cog|peo|𐎱𐎰|ts=paθi}}), {{cog|sa|पन्था}}. See also {{cog|en|find}}). {{doublet|en|panth}}.

